module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.parse_errors_are_swallowed_before_the_batch_names_the_file

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode, RungUnread, NoRungStated }

data parse_errors_are_swallowed_before_the_batch_names_the_file: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "parse_errors_are_swallowed_before_the_batch_names_the_file" as NonEmptyStr,
  rung: RungUnread { cause: NoRungStated },

  receipts: [
    "INVALID STATE: a tool prints the NAME of what failed while the CAUSE is in scope and reachable. The parse layer drops the diagnostic it is holding, and every layer above can only report that it got nothing back. The reader is told which file failed to parse and not why, so the next move is guess-and-rebuild, and the rebuild cost is a full cold cargo build of v1-compiler on the shared runner -- measured 20+ minutes per cycle including the BuildBuddy sync.",

    "HARM, MEASURED 2026-10-20 while bringing up test.claim.runner.runner_slot_cap_observe_route_witness. claim_batch printed 'claim_batch: cannot parse --entry dag/test/claim/runner/... while validating functions' (the claim_batch binary's validate_explicit_functions) and nothing else -- the message is the WHOLE report: the file's name, no cause, no position. Three dispatches were spent reasoning about syntax forms (annotated closures, then free-call spellings) against that bare filename. WHAT THIS ROW COVERS, WITH ITS OWN EXECUTED SPECIMEN (dispatch H, 2026-10-20 20:32 UTC, manager msg_b4182756 demand): a grammar-stage defect -- unterminated fn block, stray '%%' token, 'let 1 = 2' -- run through claim_batch under BOTH binaries. PRE-FIX (base parser): all three exit non-zero reporting ONLY 'claim_batch: cannot parse --entry dag/test/claim/runner/p4_parse_swallow_specimen.dag while validating functions' (located lines 0, filename-only line present). POST-FIX (this PR's parser): all three exit non-zero AND print a located 'p4_parse_swallow_specimen.dag:<line>:<col>: error: ...' line (located 1 each). The harm is measured, not asserted: the same construct, the tool that held the cause printed nothing beyond a filename. A genuine parse failure of the original witness (the unsupported closure annotation of that day) is the same class as the specimen: the ErrorNode -- message, file, byte offset -- is held in scope by parse_dag_content and dropped before any consumer sees it. WHAT IT DOES NOT COVER, and was wrongly folded into it at first drafting: the bare-FileContent collision was NEVER this class -- see THE PAIRING, which now states the re-derived truth (manager ruling, review 5588645431 word-fix).",

    "THE SWALLOW, AT THE SOURCE: src/v1/stage0/src/module_path_index/parsed_dag_file.rs parse_dag_content holds 'let result = parse(tokens, source_indices.clone()); if result.error.is_some() { return None; }'. The ParseResult.error is right THERE -- text and byte offsets -- and is discarded on the None path. The caller above (claim_batch validate_functions) maps None to a bare filename. Two layers of lossy degradation, and the one site that holds the truth throws it away. The same swallow starves EVERY consumer of parse_dag_file, not just claim_batch: module_path_index building, the discovery census, and the lenses that route through them all see 'unparseable', never 'why'.",

    "THE DISTINGUISHING FACT, and why this is earliest-boundary and not a claim_batch fix: any caller-side patch (catch Err, print more) still has nothing to print, because the cause dies at the None conversion, one frame below every consumer. A ceiling at claim_batch would leave parse_dag_file's own contract lossy for its other callers. The rung found at is the parse layer itself: the function that computes the diagnostic is the function that must not drop it.",

    "THE FIX, AS LANDED IN THIS PR: parse_dag_content renders the error's message with its located position (file:line:col through the same span/newline-index route the parse sweep carries) to stderr before collapsing to None. The Option signature deliberately does NOT change: its consumer population's mixed policies (decls_parse_only_from_disk fails closed; coproduct_reflection's census walk continues-and-excludes) mean a signature change would silently move census denominators -- a separate subject with its own witnesses, named in the function's doc comment. The per-file requirement was load-bearing: v1_src_dag_parse sweeps the WHOLE corpus (~22 min remote) where claim_batch needs one file, so the diagnostic had to live where the batch already is. NEXT TRIGGER: any change to parse_dag_content or claim_batch's validate_functions must carry the cause; any new claim file that 'cannot parse' must be answered by a positioned diagnostic in one dispatch, not by a rebuild guess-loop.",

    "THE PAIRING, RE-DERIVED AS THE MANAGER ORDERED (review 5588645431): parse_dag_content is tokenize->parse, NO resolve -- so a constructor-reference admission refusal CANNOT be what it was swallowing, and the first drafting of this row said something it could not have said. THE COLLISION IS A DISTINCT CLASS, NOT THIS ROW'S SUBJECT, AND ITS CAUSE WAS NOT DROPPED AT ANY SEED LAYER. Re-derivation: the collision error IS emitted located and in full by the RESOLVE layer -- the seed's v1_compiler_infer constructor_admission_diags_from_lookup renders 'dag/extdeps/transports/file_declared.dag:31:74: error: constructor reference admission cannot establish the declaration identity of ...' -- and the claim_batch binary's resolve_timed prints it under its own heading on a SECOND LINE. What made it look swallowed was the DISPATCH'S DISPLAY FILTER: the run grepped for PASS|FAIL|resolve failed and dropped every line after the heading -- including the located cause, which was there the whole time (the fallback grep over the FULL log matched it; the filtered display never showed it). So: the swallow fix exposes PARSE diagnostics (the cannot-parse-without-cause class above, now located); for the collision, NOTHING was dropped -- the second defect was consuming a truncated view of the tool's own output. WHAT STAYS UNFIXED, stated so the row does not read as resolved: the collision itself -- the resolve-layer ambient admission -- is unfixed in the seed and unfixed by this PR; only the qualification hides it, and its owner and dissolution are named below. Recipe lesson carried by this row: assert on the whole log, never a first-line grep of a two-line diagnostic. THE COLLISION'S OWN NATURE: bare 'FileContent' bound another module's alias (v2.extdeps.file_system.FileContent) once both modules sat in one claim closure -- a name-resolution hazard: a bare name binding a declaration from a module the file never imports, chosen by what the closure dragged in, which the operator's 2026-10-15 ruling forbids as ambient cross-module admission. OWNER: tidy-ant-251 (M1 lane, native source semantics), who took the specimen 2026-10-20 and will signal when the resolver fix is up for the repro to be re-run. THE QUALIFIED SELF-REFERENCES ARE WORKAROUNDS, NOT DISPOSITIONS: two sites carry them -- extdeps.transports.file_declared's file_output_channel_of_key (this PR) and the pre-existing extdeps.transports.file's file_output_channel_of_key. Both exist only until the resolver fix lands; their dissolution is that fix, and when it ships, BOTH qualifications should be deleted together (re-run the repro at --entry dag/test/claim/runner/runner_slot_cap_observe_route_witness.dag to confirm the bare form resolves locally again).",

    "DISPOSITION OF THIS ROW, so it does not read as more or less than it is: THE SWALLOW IS FIXED IN THE SAME PR that opened this row -- parse_dag_content now renders the diagnostic message with its located position (file:line:col through the same span/line-col route the parse sweep uses) before collapsing to None; the Option SIGNATURE stays, because its consumer population's mixed policies (fail-closed decls vs. census-continue) are a separate unreconciled subject named in the function's own doc comment. So the row stands as the RECORD of the class and its measured harm, with its repair landed; the SIGNATURE question (absence as a reading of a parse failure, per caller) is explicitly NOT closed here and remains the trigger for anyone converting those callers.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
